---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<PageLayout meta={{ title: "Gallery" }}>
	<div class="w-full">
		<h1 class="title mb-12">Gallery</h1>

		<div class="mb-12 pb-8 border-b border-gray-600">
			<p class="mb-4 text-base">
				This space is an attempt to recreate figures from research that has shaped how I think about economics and data. 
				Some are full replications, others just visual exercises to test an idea or method.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-4 text-base">
				Each figure credits the original source and, where possible, links to data and code.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-4 text-base">
				Think of it as a small, evolving gallery of economics.
			</p>
			<p class="text-sm text-gray-500 text-center mt-6 italic">
	               Last Updated: February 2026
             </p>
		</div>

		<div class="space-y-4">
			<!-- NREGS Running Variable Manipulation Test -->
			<details class="group border border-gray-700 rounded-lg">
				<summary class="cursor-pointer p-6 hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors flex items-center" style="list-style: none;">
					<span class="transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 mr-4">▼</span>
					<h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Testing for Running Variable Manipulation in NREGS Phase Allocation</h2>
				</summary>
				<div class="px-6 pb-6 space-y-6">
					<div class="mb-6">
						<img
							src="/kastkan/unmanipulated_runningvar.png"
							alt="Distribution of Planning Commission Index over Backwardness Ranks"
							class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-lg"
						/>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-400 italic">
							My replication (left) & Figure 2 from Zimmermann (2024) (right): General Distribution of Planning Commission Index over Backwardness Ranks
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="space-y-4 text-base">
						<p>
							This figure replicates Figure 2 from Zimmermann (2024) and illustrates an essential diagnostic for Fuzzy Regression Discontinuity Designs (FRDDs): the running variable must be exogenous to treatment assignment and unmanipulable by those who might benefit from it.
						</p>
						<p>
							<strong>Why this matters:</strong> In the context of the National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (NREGS), districts were phased into the program based on their Backwardness Rank, derived from the Planning Commission's Backwardness Index (2003). The index itself was constructed using data from the NSS 50th (1993–94) and 55th (1999–2000) rounds—collected well before NREGS was enacted in 2006. Consequently, districts had neither the foresight nor the incentive to manipulate the indicators underlying these scores. 
							<!--strategically misreport indicators used to generate these backwardness scores. -->	
						</p>
						<p>
                           The smooth, monotonic relationship between the Three-Parameter Index and Backwardness Rank provides both institutional and empirical reassurance that the running variable was not subject to manipulation. 
                            <!--There is no bunching or discontinuity near program thresholds, supporting the assumption that phase assignment was determined by predetermined characteristics rather than endogenous behavior. -->						</p>
						<p class="text-sm" style="color: #648fd8;">
							<strong>Source:</strong> Replication of Figure 2 from Zimmermann, L. V. (2024). Why guarantee employment? Evidence from a large Indian public-works program. Economic Development and Cultural Change, 72(4), 2031-2067.
						</p>
						<div class="flex gap-4 text-sm">
							<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/conference/2015/retrieve.php?pdfid=2907&tk=TkiyG3R8" class="text-accent hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original Paper</a>
							<a href="https://github.com/kasturikandalam/nregs-migration" class="text-accent hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">My Code & Data</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</details>


			<!-- SARSONS | COAUTHORSHIP AND TENURE -->
			 
<details class="group border border-gray-700 rounded-lg">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer p-6 hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors flex items-center" style="list-style: none;">
        <span class="transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 mr-4">▼</span>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Coauthorship, Gender, and Tenure</h2>
    </summary>
    <div class="px-6 pb-6 space-y-6">
        <div class="mb-6">
            <img
                src="/kastkan/sarsons_replication.png"
                alt="Binned scatterplot showing relationship between fraction of solo-authored papers and tenure probability, split by gender"
                class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-lg"
            />
            <p class="text-sm italic mt-2" style="color: #888;">
                My replication (above) & Figure 2 from Sarsons et al. (2021): Relationship between Paper Composition and Tenure, by Gender
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-base">
            <p>
                This figure replicates Figure 2 from Sarsons et al. (2021) and illustrates one of the paper's 
                central findings: coauthorship translates into tenure very differently for men and women in academic economics.
            </p>
            <p>
                The figure is a binned scatterplot of tenure against the fraction of papers that are solo-authored, 
                split by gender, controlling for total papers, citations, journal quality, number of coauthors, 
                and institution, year, and field fixed effects.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Why this matters:</strong> For men, paper composition is essentially irrelevant — tenure rates are flat 
                regardless of whether they coauthor or solo author (β = −0.035, SE = 0.059). For women, the 
                relationship is strongly positive (β = 0.406, SE = 0.131): women who solo author all their papers 
                have tenure rates comparable to men, while women who coauthor all their papers face an 
                approximately 37% tenure rate — far below that of comparable men. The paper argues this gap 
                is best explained by gender influencing credit attribution for coauthored work: when individual 
                contributions are unobservable, employers appear to systematically discount women's share of 
                joint output relative to men's.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm" style="color: #30a6d1;">
                <strong>Source:</strong> Replication of Figure 2 from Sarsons, H., Gërxhani, K., Reuben, E., & Schram, A. (2021). 
                Gender Differences in Recognition for Group Work. <em>Journal of Political Economy</em>, 129(1), 101–147.
            </p>
            <div class="flex gap-4 text-sm">
                <a href="https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/711401" class="text-accent hover:underline" target="_blank">Original Paper</a>
                <a href="#https://github.com/kasturikandalam/sarsons_replication" class="text-accent hover:underline" target="_blank">My Code & Data</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</details>
			

